  "final_span": "the budget, which it approved 6-3.\nCouncilman James Rogers, along with co-sponsors Councilmen Chris Wharton and Derek Kitchen, sought to continue the debate on the police hires and the proposed homeless housing project downtown at 360 W. 300 South. Last week, the council postponed that vote, so the plan for a homeless housing location remains unresolved.\nCouncilman Charlie Luke continually supported the allocation of extra funding for programs benefiting families and neighborhoods. He advocated for increased funding for youth programs and neighborhood services, which he believes are more effective at preventing problems than police intervention.\n\"We know prevention is very successful, and the police respond during the aftermath,\" Luke said. \"I would hope we would do more in terms of adding resources in our neighborhoods, addressing what our residents tell us is important to them, instead of the current tough-on-crime approach. We hear you loud and clear \u2014 you want us to be more responsive to what you say about crime in your neighborhood.\"\nHe also attempted to convince his colleagues on the council to allocate additional funds for deep-tissue road reconstruction, which costs significantly more than the less expensive asphalt mill and overlay.\n\"I understand the argument against wasting money by doing a mill and overlay, but the reality is that in the meantime, with the general decay in our city in terms of our roads, I'm concerned that when we finally do decide maybe we would do a mill and overlay, it would cost more, since the roads would have deteriorated,\" Luke said.\nHe suggested the city might also benefit from consulting with Denver or some European cities that utilize deep concrete pavement, which may be pricier initially but offers much longer-lasting alternatives to asphalt.\n\"We could do mill and overlay and have to come back two years from now or three years from now, and it would be much worse to spend the money now,\" Luke said.\nLuke finally succeeded in persuading his fellow council members to invest in asphalt mill and overlay, as the approximately $1.5 million budget is redirected toward this more economical option.\nAnother topic of debate Tuesday was the council's use of free",
